        'cat' => 'Therapy services',
        'short' => 'Counselling is talking with a trained professional about difficult feelings, life changes or challenges, and building ways to cope. It is a private, respectful space to work things through at your own pace.',
        'overview' => "People come to counselling for many reasons: stress, grief, relationship difficulties, adjusting to a diagnosis or a change in circumstances, or simply wanting to understand themselves better. A counsellor listens without judgement, helps you make sense of what you are experiencing and works with you on practical strategies.\n\nApproaches differ. Some counsellors focus on talking through experiences, others teach specific skills for managing thoughts, emotions or relationships. There is no single right method, and the most important factor in many studies is whether you feel comfortable and understood by the person you are working with.\n\nCounselling is separate from other mental health supports such as psychology or psychiatry, although the boundaries can overlap. If you are unsure which is best for you, a GP or an experienced provider can help you decide, and you can change your approach as your needs change.",
